 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot P)$ where $N$ is the number of triangles and $P$ is the average number of pixels covered per triangle. It is faster than multi-view methods by a factor of 3 in rasterization overhead, but requires more complex per-pixel logic for edge cases.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(R_x R_y R_z)$ for the output grid.
-*   **Strengths:** Reduces GPU rasterization passes by 2/3 compared to 3-axis methods. Maintains 6-separating (tunnel-free) property.
-*   **Limitations:** Logic is more complex than simple center-depth tests. Requires careful handling of edge cases where the triangle grazes a pixel.
-*   **When to use:** When rasterization cost is a bottleneck and the scene geometry allows for efficient dominant-axis selection.
-*   **When not to use:** If the geometry is extremely thin or aligned such that dominant axis selection leads to degenerate projections (though the method claims robustness via the crosshair equivalence).
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot P)$ where $N$ is the number of triangles and $P$ is the number of pixels in the projected bounding box. This is significantly faster than 3-axis methods which require $3 \times P$ processing.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V)$ where $V$ is the number of voxels in the grid.
+*   **Strengths:** Reduced computational cost due to single projection; maintains 6-separating (tunnel-free) property [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires careful handling of edge cases where the triangle is parallel to the projection plane (normal has zero component in dominant axis, though this is rare for planar triangles unless degenerate). Performance depends on the distribution of triangle orientations.
+*   **When to use:** When computational resources are limited and 6-connectivity is required. When triangles are not aligned with the principal axes, making multi-view projection redundant for many pixels.
 
 ## Background
-This method is derived from the "thinness" property of crosshair-based voxelization, as described by Laine et al. [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the equivalence between 3-axis rasterization and the crosshairs target [FBP09, CNS*11]. The key insight is that for a planar triangle, projecting along the dominant normal axis is sufficient to capture all voxels in the column, as other projection directions cannot produce additional voxels in those columns due to thinness [S1, unofficial]. This allows for a 6-separating voxelization using only one 2D projection per triangle.
+This method was introduced by Laine et al. in "A Topological Approach to Voxelization" (EGSR 2013) [S1, unofficial]. It leverages the "thinness" property of voxelizations, which states that the voxel set produced by the crosshairs target is minimal in a topological sense. The method was developed to optimize the voxelization process by reducing the number of required projections while preserving topological correctness (6-separating). It is used in applications requiring efficient voxelization of triangle meshes, such as volume rendering and collision detection.
 
